Eric Rescorla <ekr@rtfm.com> writes:=0A=
=0A=
>In my experience, there are four major scenarios for diagnosing this kind =
of=0A=
>failure. Under the assumption that you control one end, the other end can =
be:=0A=
>=0A=
>1. A live endpoint.=0A=
>2. A testing endpoint someone has put up.=0A=
>3. An endpoint that someone is actively working on with you.=0A=
>4. An endpoint you control (e.g., you're running it on your own machine).=
=0A=
>=0A=
>If this is a debug-only feature, then it won't be available in case #1, =
=0A=
=0A=
I have the feeling the people who have commented on this were talking from=
=0A=
real-world experience, and in the example I gave it was exactly case #1.  T=
his=0A=
was a live, large-scale production environment by a major ecommerce=0A=
organisation (details fudged somewhat to avoid identifying anyone, but=0A=
everyone here would know the name), and the only way to get things working =
was=0A=
to spend several weeks randomly tweaking every conceivable option on the=0A=
client until things started working, because the only thing the server woul=
d=0A=
say was "Handshake failure".  The client-side organisation still has no ide=
a=0A=
what made things work, they've narrowed it down by trial and error to about=
=0A=
half a dozen things they had to change, but that's it.=0A=
=0A=
If they'd been able to get the server operators to turn on extended-alert f=
or=0A=
even just a single handshake it would have avoided several weeks' effort an=
d a=0A=
fix that even now is pure guesswork.=0A=
=0A=
>For the same reason, it's not really that helpful in case #3, because you =
can=0A=
>just ask the person you're working with to read the logs, =0A=
=0A=
Except that these people are EDI companies, not TLS experts.  They have=0A=
neither the expertise nor the inclination to help debug TLS issues.  What t=
hey=0A=
will do is enable debug on the server so the client can sort things out, bu=
t=0A=
they're not going to devote any effort to sorting out the problem at their=
=0A=
end.=0A=
=0A=
Note that temporarily enabling debug on a live endpoint isn't a big issue,=
=0A=
everything will continue to operate as before except for the one client tha=
t=0A=
requests debug-level alerts, and that knows what it's up for because it=0A=
explicitly asked for it.=0A=
=0A=
>so this leaves case #2, =0A=
=0A=
Actually it leaves 1, 2, and 3.  4 is kinda pathological, so really the=0A=
problem is "all of the cases".=0A=
=0A=
Peter.=0A=

